1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:07,890
>> In 1874, the English economist William Stanley Jevons printed a puzzle in a book.

2
00:00:07,970 --> 00:00:17,390
>> He asked: which two numbers, multiplied together, make 8,616,460,799?

3
00:00:17,470 --> 00:00:22,960
>> And then: "I think it unlikely that anyone but myself will ever know."

4
00:00:23,040 --> 00:00:24,430
>> Confident.

5
00:00:24,510 --> 00:00:31,080
>> Well, multiplying two numbers is easy. Finding them again from the answer can be very hard.

6
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:36,230
>> And lopsided problems like his now guard every secure web page you open.

7
00:00:36,310 --> 00:00:41,350
>> They're what let two strangers agree on a secret while everyone listens.

8
00:00:41,430 --> 00:00:47,900
This is the story of public-key cryptography, and it was invented twice.

9
00:00:51,100 --> 00:00:56,420
>> For most of history, sending a secret message meant sharing a secret key first.

10
00:00:56,500 --> 00:01:02,460
>> And the same key locks the message and unlocks it, so whoever has it can read everything.

11
00:01:02,540 --> 00:01:04,580
>> So the key is the weak point.

12
00:01:04,660 --> 00:01:10,800
>> Exactly. It had to travel safely, by trusted courier or at a face-to-face meeting.

13
00:01:10,880 --> 00:01:15,420
>> And every pair of people who want to talk privately needs a key of their own.

14
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:18,390
>> Ten people need 45 keys.

15
00:01:18,470 --> 00:01:23,530
>> A thousand people need 499,500.

16
00:01:23,610 --> 00:01:26,530
>> Half a million keys to deliver.

17
00:01:26,610 --> 00:01:33,180
>> In 1976, two researchers at Stanford gave this a name: the key distribution problem.

18
00:01:33,260 --> 00:01:41,060
>> And their answer sounds impossible: strangers agreeing on a key in full view of everyone.

19
00:01:43,660 --> 00:01:52,350
>> In 1974, Whitfield Diffie, an independent researcher, was advised to meet a young Stanford professor, Martin Hellman.

20
00:01:52,430 --> 00:01:56,670
>> They'd planned a half-hour meeting. It lasted many hours.

21
00:01:56,750 --> 00:02:02,040
>> In November 1976, they published New Directions in Cryptography.

22
00:02:02,120 --> 00:02:07,610
>> Which opens: "We stand today on the brink of a revolution in cryptography."

23
00:02:07,690 --> 00:02:09,660
>> That's quite a first line.

24
00:02:09,740 --> 00:02:14,580
>> It held up: they won the 2015 Turing Award for it.

25
00:02:14,660 --> 00:02:18,000
>> So what was the revolution? Splitting the key in two.

26
00:02:18,080 --> 00:02:24,040
>> One key locks, a different key unlocks, and knowing the first doesn't give you the second.

27
00:02:24,120 --> 00:02:30,540
>> So you could publish your locking key like a phone number, and anyone could send you messages only you can read.

28
00:02:30,620 --> 00:02:34,560
>> Anyone can close the lock. Only you can open it.

29
00:02:34,640 --> 00:02:41,910
>> But for that they needed a trapdoor one-way function: easy to compute, hard to undo, unless you hold a secret.

30
00:02:41,990 --> 00:02:46,730
>> And they didn't have one. The problem, they wrote, was "still largely open".

31
00:02:46,810 --> 00:02:50,030
>> So the revolution came with a hole in the middle.

32
00:02:50,110 --> 00:02:57,550
>> A hole, yes. But they did have a way to agree on a key in public, building on ideas from Ralph Merkle.

33
00:02:57,630 --> 00:03:02,750
>> And Hellman later said the method should carry Merkle's name too.

34
00:03:05,350 --> 00:03:11,760
>> Let's try it in paint first. Alice and Bob agree, in public, on a starting colour: yellow.

35
00:03:11,840 --> 00:03:16,830
>> Each mixes in a secret colour of their own: red for Alice, blue for Bob.

36
00:03:16,910 --> 00:03:22,230
>> They swap the mixtures in the open, where Eve, listening in, sees them too.

37
00:03:22,310 --> 00:03:28,130
>> Then each stirs in their own secret again, and they both end up with the same brown.

38
00:03:28,210 --> 00:03:33,920
And Eve has the yellow and both mixtures, but she can't pull the secret colours back out.

39
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:38,820
>> Is the paint picture fair, though? It seems a little too perfect.

40
00:03:38,900 --> 00:03:45,270
>> It's fair until here: paint can never be unmixed, but the maths can, by trying every secret.

41
00:03:45,350 --> 00:03:50,440
>> So the defence is sheer numbers: far too many secrets to try.

42
00:03:50,520 --> 00:03:56,090
>> Right. The real version uses clock arithmetic. Picture a clock with 23 hours.

43
00:03:56,170 --> 00:04:03,630
>> Start at 1 and keep multiplying by 5, keeping only the remainder after dividing by 23.

44
00:04:03,710 --> 00:04:09,730
>> The hand jumps 5, 2, 10, 4, 20 with no pattern you can easily see.

45
00:04:09,810 --> 00:04:12,000
>> It looks almost random.

46
00:04:12,080 --> 00:04:19,620
>> Stepping forward is easy. Working back to how many steps it took is the discrete logarithm problem, and it's hard.

47
00:04:19,700 --> 00:04:24,860
>> Now the exchange. Everyone, Eve included, knows 23 and 5.

48
00:04:24,940 --> 00:04:29,960
>> Alice secretly picks 3. Three steps land on 10, and she sends 10.

49
00:04:30,040 --> 00:04:36,450
>> Bob secretly picks 7. Seven steps land on 17, and he sends 17.

50
00:04:36,530 --> 00:04:43,820
>> Then Alice raises Bob's 17 to her secret power, 3, and Bob raises Alice's 10 to his, 7.

51
00:04:43,900 --> 00:04:51,840
>> And both get 14, because both have really taken 3 times 7, or 21, steps from the start.

52
00:04:51,920 --> 00:04:55,080
>> Same steps, different order. Just like the paint.

53
00:04:55,160 --> 00:05:02,530
>> Meanwhile Eve heard 23, 5, 10 and 17. To reach 14, she needs 3 or 7.

54
00:05:02,610 --> 00:05:12,980
>> With 23 hours she could try them all, so real exchanges use a prime of at least 2,048 bits: 617 digits.

55
00:05:15,580 --> 00:05:21,920
Key exchange was a breakthrough. But a true public lock, one that anyone could close, was still missing.

56
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:29,160
>> At MIT, Ron Rivest, Adi Shamir and Leonard Adleman spent about a year hunting for one.

57
00:05:29,240 --> 00:05:34,980
>> Rivest and Shamir proposed ideas, and Adleman, the mathematician, kept breaking them.

58
00:05:35,060 --> 00:05:36,670
>> Somebody has to.

59
00:05:36,750 --> 00:05:44,290
Then, in April 1977, after a Passover evening at a student's house, Rivest couldn't sleep.

60
00:05:44,370 --> 00:05:48,460
>> By daybreak, the story goes, he had much of the paper written.

61
00:05:48,540 --> 00:05:54,680
>> It's named RSA after their initials, and it starts with two prime numbers.

62
00:05:54,760 --> 00:06:02,630
>> Let's build a tiny one. Take the primes 61 and 53, and multiply them: 3,233.

63
00:06:02,710 --> 00:06:11,350
>> Pick 17 as the power that locks. From the primes, we can work out the power that unlocks: 413.

64
00:06:11,430 --> 00:06:17,940
>> And the public key is just 3,233 and 17. Anyone can have it.

65
00:06:18,020 --> 00:06:27,730
Right. To send Bob 65, raise it to the power of 17 and keep the remainder after dividing by 3,233.

66
00:06:27,810 --> 00:06:35,400
>> That power has 31 digits, but only the remainder, 2,790, travels.

67
00:06:35,480 --> 00:06:44,490
>> Bob raises that to the power of 413, divides by 3,233 again, and out comes 65.

68
00:06:44,570 --> 00:06:47,110
>> Wait, straight back to 65?

69
00:06:47,190 --> 00:06:50,950
>> Exactly. The two powers undo each other, if you know both.

70
00:06:51,030 --> 00:06:58,190
>> And finding 413 needs the two primes. The public key shows only their product.

71
00:06:58,270 --> 00:07:05,940
>> Real systems also add random padding. Bare RSA like this is just a demonstration.

72
00:07:08,540 --> 00:07:15,560
>> So it all rests on a lopsided pair of jobs: multiplying is easy, and factoring is hard.

73
00:07:15,640 --> 00:07:20,550
>> How hard, though? 3,233 doesn't look so scary.

74
00:07:20,630 --> 00:07:26,900
>> It isn't. Try dividing by each prime in turn, and the 16th, 53, works.

75
00:07:26,980 --> 00:07:36,090
>> Jevons's number needs thousands of tries. It's 89,681 times 96,079.

76
00:07:36,170 --> 00:07:39,130
>> Charles Busk found that in 1889.

77
00:07:39,210 --> 00:07:41,870
>> So much for "anyone but myself".

78
00:07:41,950 --> 00:07:54,720
>> Then, in August 1977, Martin Gardner described RSA in Scientific American, with a challenge: a message locked with a 129-digit number.

79
00:07:54,800 --> 00:07:57,890
>> With a $100 prize for unlocking it.

80
00:07:57,970 --> 00:08:07,060
>> Rivest estimated that, with the best methods of the day, a 125-digit number would take 40 quadrillion years.

81
00:08:07,140 --> 00:08:09,930
>> Forty quadrillion. That sounds safe.

82
00:08:10,010 --> 00:08:19,700
>> It wasn't. Better methods came, and in 1994 about 600 volunteers, with some 1,600 computers, cracked it.

83
00:08:19,780 --> 00:08:24,520
>> The message read: "The magic words are squeamish ossifrage."

84
00:08:24,600 --> 00:08:29,840
>> An ossifrage is a vulture, by the way. The name means bone-breaker.

85
00:08:29,920 --> 00:08:38,810
>> So keys grew. Public factoring records are under 300 digits; a 2,048-bit key has 617.

86
00:08:38,890 --> 00:08:47,440
>> And no one has proved that factoring is hard. There's just no fast way known on ordinary computers.

87
00:08:50,040 --> 00:08:55,680
>> Now run the same maths the other way, and you get something just as useful: a signature.

88
00:08:55,760 --> 00:09:02,850
>> Diffie and Hellman proposed digital signatures in 1976, and RSA made them work.

89
00:09:02,930 --> 00:09:09,520
>> Bob locks a message with his private 413. Anyone can open it with his public 17.

90
00:09:09,600 --> 00:09:12,760
>> But if anyone can open it, what's the point?

91
00:09:12,840 --> 00:09:24,780
>> The point is who could have locked it. Signing 1977 gives 788, and 788 to the power of 17 gives back 1977.

92
00:09:24,860 --> 00:09:32,100
>> Only the holder of 413 could have made 788, so it proves who signed.

93
00:09:32,180 --> 00:09:36,900
>> In practice you sign a short fingerprint of the message, called a hash.

94
00:09:36,980 --> 00:09:42,970
>> And signatures close a gap we skipped over in key exchange: an attacker in the middle.

95
00:09:43,050 --> 00:09:48,990
>> Mallory sits between Alice and Bob and runs one exchange with each, posing as the other.

96
00:09:49,070 --> 00:09:56,390
>> Each thinks they share a key with the other. Both actually share one with Mallory, who reads everything.

97
00:09:56,470 --> 00:09:58,660
>> And neither of them would notice.

98
00:09:58,740 --> 00:10:03,480
>> No. Plain Diffie–Hellman can't tell who is really on the other end.

99
00:10:03,560 --> 00:10:11,630
>> On the web, the proof is a certificate: the site's public key, signed by an authority your browser trusts.

100
00:10:14,230 --> 00:10:18,370
>> Now for the twist, kept secret for more than twenty years.

101
00:10:18,450 --> 00:10:26,860
>> In January 1970, at Britain's GCHQ, James Ellis wrote a secret report on "non-secret encryption".

102
00:10:26,940 --> 00:10:34,080
>> The idea came from a wartime Bell Labs paper: the receiver adds noise to a call, then removes it.

103
00:10:34,160 --> 00:10:36,700
>> So the receiver helps lock the message.

104
00:10:36,780 --> 00:10:41,920
>> Right. Ellis argued that it was possible, but he couldn't see a way to build it.

105
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:49,570
>> Then, in September 1973, a young mathematician, Clifford Cocks, joined and heard about the idea.

106
00:10:49,650 --> 00:10:56,290
>> Cocks knew number theory. He chose prime factoring, and quickly found what we now call RSA.

107
00:10:56,370 --> 00:10:58,690
>> He didn't even write it down at the time.

108
00:10:58,770 --> 00:11:01,260
>> Four years before MIT.

109
00:11:01,340 --> 00:11:08,480
>> And in 1974, his colleague Malcolm Williamson found what we now call Diffie–Hellman key exchange.

110
00:11:08,560 --> 00:11:16,730
>> With a military focus and little computing power, GCHQ found no use for it, and it stayed secret.

111
00:11:16,810 --> 00:11:23,220
It was made public in 1997, when Cocks gave a talk on 18 December.

112
00:11:23,300 --> 00:11:28,540
>> James Ellis had died on 25 November, a month too soon.

113
00:11:28,620 --> 00:11:36,040
>> And as far as anyone knows, the Stanford and MIT teams found it all on their own.

114
00:11:38,640 --> 00:11:42,730
>> So what actually happens when you open a secure web page today?

115
00:11:42,810 --> 00:11:49,770
>> Your browser and the server run a fresh key exchange, often on elliptic curves rather than a clock.

116
00:11:49,850 --> 00:11:55,720
>> The server signs the exchange, and its certificate shows that the signature really is the site's.

117
00:11:55,800 --> 00:11:58,170
>> That's the answer to Mallory.

118
00:11:58,250 --> 00:12:04,890
>> Public-key maths is slow, so it only sets up a key; a fast ordinary cipher carries the page.

119
00:12:04,970 --> 00:12:12,480
>> And the exchange keys are thrown away afterwards, so a key stolen later can't unlock today's traffic.

120
00:12:12,560 --> 00:12:23,730
>> RSA keys usually have 2,048 bits or more. On elliptic curves, 256 bits does as well as 3,072.

121
00:12:26,330 --> 00:12:32,570
In 1994, Peter Shor showed how a quantum computer could factor huge numbers quickly.

122
00:12:32,650 --> 00:12:39,770
>> And his method solves discrete logarithms too, so it would break RSA and Diffie–Hellman alike.

123
00:12:39,850 --> 00:12:42,190
>> Both of them? Should we be worried?

124
00:12:42,270 --> 00:12:51,230
>> Not yet. No such machine exists: demonstrations have factored only 15 and 21, helped by knowing the answer.

125
00:12:51,310 --> 00:12:56,550
>> Breaking real keys might take millions of qubits, because of error correction.

126
00:12:56,630 --> 00:13:03,620
>> The worry is that a message recorded today could be decrypted later: harvest now, decrypt later.

127
00:13:03,700 --> 00:13:13,840
>> So in August 2024, NIST, the US standards institute, published three post-quantum standards: one for keys, two for signatures.

128
00:13:13,920 --> 00:13:20,490
>> The key one, ML-KEM, rests on lattices: problems thought to resist quantum attacks.

129
00:13:20,570 --> 00:13:28,320
>> And Chrome and Firefox already mix it into their key exchange, alongside a classic elliptic curve.

130
00:13:28,920 --> 00:13:35,760
>> So strangers can agree on a key in public, because some sums are easy one way and hard the other.

131
00:13:35,840 --> 00:13:39,750
>> A lock can be public, if only its owner knows the trapdoor.

132
00:13:39,830 --> 00:13:43,290
>> And run backwards, the same maths signs.

133
00:13:43,370 --> 00:13:49,260
>> Jevons was wrong about his number, but right that some sums are far easier done than undone.

134
00:13:49,340 --> 00:13:58,480
>> And every secure connection begins that way: two strangers, in public, agreeing on a secret only they know.
